Andy Glew wrote:
> 
> I'm sure that I'll be flamed royally for the above. All I am doing is synthesizing
> what I have been told by CFD experts.

Hmmm.... I don't see any reason to flame you, but I think I will 
take some time to disagree....  Maybe a lot of time....



<-----Activate Professor Mode----->


> Here is what I have gathered from talking to
> Computational Fluid Dynamics people:
> 
> CFD codes are used
>     a) in ocean models
>     b) in graphics - movies, etc.
>     c) in nuclear bomb blast modelling - the "top secret" area
> you mention above.

(a) Ocean models do not represent a particularly large amount of
    revenue for vendors of high-performance computers -- much less
    that the associated field of weather modelling (which is dynamically
    related, but quite different from the view of performance modelling).

    Ocean models certainly are CFD codes, but they are woefully
    behind the times in their formulation and numerical methods.
    There are two reasons for this:
      (a.1) The computational task is so immense that it
            seems silly to add more numerical intensity 
            to the problem.  (There is some truth in this,
            but making the problems harder to solve is 
            still the right answer.)
      (a.2) Ocean modellers in academia, defense, and government
            research labs have found themselves unable to give
            up their dependence on 1960's numerical modelling
            technology.  This is reinforced by a culture that
            does not allow researchers to ask if results coming
            out of numerical models are correct and that denies 
            funding to researchers who wish to import
            significant amounts of modern CFD into their work.
            
            (Yes, there is a fair amount of disgust implicit in
            the previous paragraph, brought on by bitter personal
            experience.  But it did bring me into computer architecture,
            so it was not an entirely wasted 14 years.)

    This backwardness in numerical methods is part of why these codes
    run so well on vector machines.  Correspondingly, the changes
    required to modernize the codes would decrease the suitability
    for vector machines and increase the suitability for cached machines.


(b) I do not believe that CFD codes used in movies or graphics
    represent a significant amount of revenue, or that they are
    intrinsically tightly linked with the performance characteristics
    of the codes that are important (I will get to that eventually).
    The people that I have talked to in this area have often "fiddled"
    with the models in order to generate the desired appearance, and
    this makes comparisons with other sub-fields difficult.


(c) CFD codes are certainly used as part of nuclear weapon simulation.
    (By the way, this was *not* the highly secret area that I was
    referring to earlier where vector machines still have a large
    performance advantage.)   There are several different aspects
    of weapon simulation that are important, but it is hard to tell
    how accurate a picture can be gained from entirely "outside the
    fence".

    The most obvious application of CFD is with the high-resolution
    sPPM code used for hydrodynamics with intense shocks.  I do not
    have any first-hand experience with running this on vector machines,
    but it certainly runs very well on scalar machines, delivering 
    about 1 TFLOPS sustained performance on both the ASCI "Blue Pacific"
    machine at Livermore (a group of three very large IBM SP's -- almost
    6000 cpus in total), and the ASCI "Blue Mountain" machine at Los 
    Alamos (a cluster of 48 128p SGI Origin2000's).  Newer versions of
    the sPPM code have been developed to run on generic clusters of
    SMPs with excellent scaling and performance.

    Other applications of CFD to nuclear weapons design are less closely
    associated with the economically important applications of CFD, which
    I will actually get to next.


(d) Andy's survey missed most of the areas of CFD that are economically
    important.  Several of these are:
    (d.1) Aerospace engineering -- CFD testing of airplanes, helicopters,
          missiles, etc.
    (d.2) Automotive engineering -- drag coefficient modelling, under-hood
          heat transport, and noise modelling.
    (d.3) Petroleum Reservoir modelling
    (d.4) "generic" CAE -- low-speed airflow and heat transport for 
          virtually every device that needs cooling, especially including
          computers and HVAC ("Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning").
    (d.4) Weather Modelling -- both local-area and global.  The economically
          important applications are in short-term forecasting, while long-
          term simulations are important in the smaller research area.


(e) Andy's survey also missed a couple of areas that, while not of 
    economic importance comparable to the items in (d), are comparable to
    the areas he listed:
    (e.1) Groundwater modelling -- similar to Petroleum Reservoir modelling
          in a number of ways.
    (e.2) Air pollution modelling -- not really the same as weather modelling
          because of the specialized interests.
    (e.3) Biomedical CFD -- a really tough area of increasing interest
          (but not yet associated with a lot of $$$).
    (e.4) Combustion modelling -- another really tough area of increasing
          interest.  Important applications are in internal combustion engines,
          fluidized bed reactors (go look it up), and other chemical reactors.
    (e.5) Pipe transport -- simulation of drag and flow characteristics for
          fluid flow in pipes.  I am not sure how much of this is done
by 
          simulation and how much is done by looking up the answers in books.
          (After all, people have been pushing fluids through pipes for
a long
          time, so most of the parameter space has been pretty
thoroughly charted.)

I probably missed someone's favorite application area, and I apologize
in advance.
Blame it on the nasty flu bug that decided to ruin my Christmas vacation....




> 
> CFD codes still run best on vector machines.

Some of them run very well on vector machines, especially [a,d2,d4], but
most of the economically important applications run quite well on workstations.
Interestingly, the "up-n-coming" areas (involving shock capturing, adaptive
meshes, non-Newtonian/anisotropic fluids, and other fun things) generally
run really *badly* on vector machines, and relatively well on scalar machines.
I say "relatively well" because although there is so much work to do per
element that cache reuse is very good, there are significant problems with
data-dependent control flow exposing functional unit latencies and branch
mispredict penalties, so the IPC is not as high as one might get from a
more vector-like code.

The most popular (in the U.S.) Petroleum Reservoir models (d3) are run 
exclusively on scalar systems.



> There was a research funding trend, from circa
> 1985 to present, where the US gov't strongly encouraged running such
> scientific codes on MPP (massively parallel systems), basically discouraging
> use of vector machines.

Whose fault was that anyway?  I am really curious, because I was just a peon
in 1985, and wonder who it was on the advisory panels of the NSF and DARPA
who pushed so hard to fund MPP machines of various sorts.  It certainly 
wasted an incredible amount of many people's time -- porting applications
to one incompatible system after another as the vendors "bit the dust".



> My CFD contacts know of no world-class CFD code that was
> voluntarily ported to an MPP, or microprocessor machine.  I.e. given their
> druthers, CFD codes would all continue to be written
> on vector machines. The only reasons CFD codes have been ported
> to non-vector machines has been
> (a) because of research grants to do just that - grants not
>     to improve CFD, but just to do the port
> (b) because no other machines were available.

Lots of CFD codes have been ported to microprocessor-based systems, both
uniprocessor and multiprocessor.  Much of the economically important 
application of CFD (especially d4) is done almost exclusively on 
workstations.

There are a couple of vendors who refuse to change their solvers,
so their codes still run much better on vector machines, but even
then, they are not cost-effective there.

Concerning "because no other machines were available", there is
a simple reason -- the vector machines have been too expensive,
and have been losing their absolute performance edge year after 
year.  In the early 1990's a colleague of mine got about $300k
to purchase computational resources for ocean modelling.  There
was absolutely no doubt that the best way to spend that money
was to purchase 5-6 well-equipped IBM RS/6000's with adequate
memory and lots of disk.  Using the money to purchase Cray time
or a "mini-supercomputer" would have been silly even then, and
the microprocessor-based systems have closed the gap quite a
bit in the intervening 7-8 years.  (Interestingly, my colleague
was forced to purchase a very small CM-200 with his money (don't
you love academic politics), and it basically sat idle for its 
entire life.  Some people with extra time on their hands finally 
got one simple code running at the speed of a single IBM work-
station.  None of the big codes were ever ported, as far as I know.)

Right now, major CFD codes are available for scalar systems
in both SMP and MPI versions, mostly with excellent scalability, 
and with price/performance that is certainly comparable to that 
of vector machines (depending on how you treat some of NEC's 
pricing).  The scalar machines have the advantage of significantly 
better performance than the vector machines for more complicated 
kernels, such as those used in combustion simulation for internal 
combustion engines.

Sure, I would rather have the 600 MFLOPS sustained performance
of a Cray T90 for my ocean models, but that system costs over
$1 million per processor, and it is only about 5x the speed of
my IBM RS/6000-397 workstation (about $20k?).  Five times the
performance for fifty times the price is not very appealing,
unless you are rich enough that $1 million is in the realm of
"discretionary funds".


> Because of the US emphasis on non-vector machines, US researchers
> are basically incapable of running detailed world weather and climate
> models. The really coarse stuff, maybe; accurate stuff, no.

I think that this is a completely bogus statement.  Weather codes can
be made to run very effectively on many classes of systems (although
message-passing ports are relatively expensive in people-effort).

Although I have only been peripherally involved with this, I would
claim that the U.S. weather modelling community had access to world-
class computational resources with available non-vector machines,
but their vector bias led them to discount that option as viable.
For example, Wesley Jones had MM5 running at better than 10 GFLOPS 
three years ago on a 128-cpu SGI Origin2000 -- I do not beleive
that any vector machines of the era offered significantly better 
performance at any price.  The global codes scale less well, but
still provide reasonable performance for an environment with some
degree of task parallelism (i.e. all shared resource centers).
Even the small T42 models delivered 2 GFLOPS on a 32-cpu Origin2000,
and I expect that the larger cases scale much better -- though I have
been unable to come up with any quantitative data on those high-
resolution cases.

The U.S. weather community could also have succeeded if they had been
able to buy Japanese vector machines.  I do not believe that NCAR
really needed vector machines to get their work done, but there is no
doubt that the SX-4's that they wanted could have worked very well.
What they got instead of the 4 32p SX-4's over a 5 year period was
a far less useful collection of machines of uneven quality and
imperfect compatibility.  

So it was a combination of *pro-vector* bias and political protectionism 
that prevented NCAR from achieving success in the manner that they desired.



