You think that the MIPS chickens are the worst?  There are a number of
the top 6 microprocessor families that could fight for "worst" for
scientific and technical computing, but MIPS would have a hard time
competing for that title.  

Here is how I see it for "real" servers:
	---------------------------------------------
	Machine				SPECfp_rate95 @ 8 cpus
	---------------------------------------------
	Compaq GS140/6/700			3134
	SGI Origin2000/300			2206
	HP N4000/440				2075
	Sun Enterprise 3500		1956 
	IBM RS/6000 9076-N80		1877
	Dell 8450					~700 (McCalpin's estimate)
	---------------------------------------------

The Compaq "Wildfire" system due out in February (?) should have similar results
(a little bit better due to the better memory system -- maybe 3300-3350?).

SGI will have a 400 MHz version of the Origin2000 "real soon now", and then
a next generation product later in the year.

HP has a new server in the works, but I don't know very much about it.
The expected boost in CPU MHz is small, but they could make gains in 
scalability (which is suffering pretty badly by the 8p point).

Sun has Ultra3's coming along, but I don't know much about their 8-way config.

IBM's entry in the list uses a pretty slow version of the Power3
processor -- 
the next version should be around the top of the list.

The Dell system with 8 550 MHz PentiumIII/Xeon/1 MB is the only 8p IA32
machine that I can get info on.  Scaling results are estimated from 2-way
SPECfp_rate95 results for the same processor.




> PHB: You want how much for a computer to predict the weather?  Ha!  Look at my
> computer, does letters and everything else I want.  Your old Cray couldn't even
> do letters.  Still, to keep you happy, I'll get you 2 new PCs, that ought to
> solve your problems.

Interesting example, given that there is no real reason to run weather codes
on vector machines.  When you configure a real model with significant physics
(or chemistry), the bandwidth requirements of these codes are actually quite
low on machines with reasonable caches.  More cache bandwidth would be nice,
but the T90 was only about 10x faster than an old uniprocessor Origin2000,
and about 40x as expensive.  This is not a stupid "value proposition",
but 
it is certainly not a strong one!  

Here are some timings on the MM5 weather model:
	http://www.mmm.ucar.edu/mm5/mm5v2/mm5v2-timing.html
The bottom line is that the Compaq DS20 is almost 4 times as fast as a
Cray Y/MP, which makes it about as fast as a Cray T90.  Now which one is 
the chicken and which one is the ox?  (The price per processor ratio is
about 100:1 in this case.)

For CCM3, some data on the NCAR web site suggests that a Cray Y/MP is
a whopping 4x faster than a 300 MHz Pentium II.  Comparing a modern 
vector machine with a modern scalar machine would not be any prettier
than it was with MM5.

For weather codes, you can't even complain that they are intrinsically
serial -- Wesley Jones has shown speedups of ~100x on 128p for MM5 (with
10-15 GFLOPS sustained performance) and has reported 2 GFLOPS sustained
performance on 32p (1.15 GFLOPS on 16p).



> Scientist: (Says nothing, it doesn't matter since what he needs no longer is
> produced. At least he can use the PC to re-do his resume.)

There are still high-end vector machines out there, it is just currently 
expensive to purchase one in the U.S.A....



