> pulling the plow as their oxen were 10 years ago or whatever,
> they don't feel that bad off, but they have no idea how
> strong today's oxen would be if they were still being bred...

In an earlier edition of Computer Architecture A Qauntitative
Approach, H&P dealt with vector processors (the section seems
to have been omitted from my later edition) and compared the
performance of an idealized vector processing version of DLS
to a pipelined scaler version. Their conclusion was that the
scaler version could perform vector operations at comparable
speed to the vectorized version.

The advantage seemed to be due to setup and iteration times
in the vectorized version, which H&P implied were common
limitations of vectorized architectures, and to the fact that
vectorization was only usefull for a fraction of the total
code base (Amdahl's law at work) so that the scaler version
could catch up either during the setup or iteration latency
(using loop unrolling) or on the runs of non-vector code.

It seems to me that the real harm done by the dominance of
comodity architectures is not due to the demise of vector
processors (which may prove to be highly exagerated), but to
the lack of any demand for really-high-functional-unit-count
systems. With enough functional units (and enough read/write
ports on a large register file) we could see large vector
operations done in few cycles again.

- Jeff Dutky



