In article <3868D97F.B0F80D63@austin.rr.com>, John  <john@mccalpin.com> wrote:
[big snip...]
> Interesting example, given that there is no real reason to run weather codes
> on vector machines.  When you configure a real model with significant physics
> (or chemistry), the bandwidth requirements of these codes are actually quite
> low on machines with reasonable caches.  More cache bandwidth would be nice,
> but the T90 was only about 10x faster than an old uniprocessor Origin2000,
> and about 40x as expensive.  This is not a stupid "value proposition",
> but 
> it is certainly not a strong one!  
> 
> Here are some timings on the MM5 weather model:
> 	http://www.mmm.ucar.edu/mm5/mm5v2/mm5v2-timing.html

These are dinky little domains, not really realistic of production
applications, imnho.  Still...

> The bottom line is that the Compaq DS20 is almost 4 times as fast as a
> Cray Y/MP, which makes it about as fast as a Cray T90.  Now which one is 
> the chicken and which one is the ox?  (The price per processor ratio is
> about 100:1 in this case.)

And that, given that MM5 is more nearly vector-machine laid-out
than not.

I started benchmarking on our new SP, using a benchmark derived from
our daily forecast runs ( MM5 v2.7, on a realistic domain:  30 levels, 
with a horizontal grid approx 120 by 90), and wasn't impressed by the 
performace  on our SP vis-a-vis similarly-clocked O-2000's.  With
less than two weeks' effort re-writing several of the more expensive 
routines, I  improved the performance by more than a factor of 2 on
uniprocessors, and improved the scalability at the same time (fewer, 
but better balanced, parallel sections).

Weather modeling is *ripe* for really clean microprocessor-parallel
implementations, since most of the computational work consists in
getting each vertical column right (and that work is trivially-parallel
w.r.t. the horizontal grid).  Ditto for air pollution models.  That's
one thing I'm hoping for out of the upcoming Weather Research and
Forecasting model (WRF).

<rant>
On the other hand, a real problem is that the data distribution
approach chosen for the message-passing version of MM5V3 shoots
many of us in the foot when we want to couple MM5 with other 
environmental models (air pollution, ground-water/land-surface,
lake/estuary/stream, ocean, ...) to create cooperating-process 
complex environmental modeling  systems.  There is no reason to
believe that the other models will respect the MM5 data decomposition
(or even that they are running on some machine at the same site --
we like it better to have MM5 running on our "air" group's machine(s)
here in NC, coupled to TOPLATS land-surface/hydrology running on one
of the Ga Tech hydrology group's machines.
</rant>




