Nikola Milutinovic wrote:
> 
> Ed Wensell III wrote:
> >
> > Allan Hawdon wrote:
> > >
> > > I've been running sys_check on an ES40 with Tru64 v4.0F and
> > > one of its recommendations is to enable fsck on / & /usr in fstab.
> > > These are advfs filesystems and I'm wary of this as I have the
> > > feeling that fsck is only for ufs filesystems. Am I just being silly
> > > here?
> >
> > That's if they are UFS. My understanding is that AdvFS is a
> > transaction-log type filesystem and therefore doesn't need fsck. It's
> > kinda like having it's own fsck built in.
> 
> It does have "verify", which can bring AdvFS to some sort of consistency even if
> it should fall out of tracks. There is also "salvage" for recovering of a
> domain, stomped by "mkfdmn".
> 
> The only situation where I've had the "accident" on AdvFS was when power was
> bad, UPS was missing and Mylex RAID (KZPSC) controller was set with write-back
> cache. Well, it had to crash.

Also, if it goes really bad, you can use dbx to set a AdvFS parameter in
the kernel. This will make the OS ignore the fact that the filesystem is
damaged, and you'll be able to rescue what's left.
You _will_ experience crashes in this process, when you try to read
what's not there, but for the rest you'll succeed.

I've used this a couple of times with good results on crashed machines.


