"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
> 
> Interested in WildFire? Check out Shannon Knows Compaq Volume 6 Number 26,
> now posted for all and sundry in your choice of Adobe Acrobat PDF and HTML
> at http://www.acersoft.com

Shannon may know Compaq, but he obviously has not been paying
attention to the rest of the industry....

In the aforementioned article, Shannon writes:
	"Theoretically, a customer could purchase a uniprocessor GS80
	 system for under ~$100K, and then, over time, fortify the 
	 platform with as many as 31 additional CPUs to yield a 
	 full-blown GS320 server. Such a "get in the front door cheap"
	 strategy is unique to Compaq; the entry price for Sun's 
	 much-vaunted Ultra Enterprise 10K server is close to ~$1M."

"Unique"???  Not quite.

This looks like an SGI Origin2000, just 3.5 years later and not
as scalable....



OK, specific issues:

    "The basic building block in a WildFire system is the QBB, 
     or Quad-Building Block. The QBB is a rack-mount module that 
     supports four Alpha EV67 CPUs, 16GB of memory, and eight
     PCI buses."

	Four-cpu nodes are completely reasonable, depending on 
	how the cache coherence protocol is handled, but it
	looks like the same mistake SGI made with the Origin2000
	design -- too much I/O.  

	Does a 32-cpu system really need 64 high-speed PCI busses?   
	Maybe for a TPC-C setup, or some specialized data warehousing 
	operation.  For most customers this will just be a waste of money.
	For comparison, the IBM S80 industry-leading TPC-C result used
	just 18 high speed RAID adapters.


    "Compaq engineers claim that the low-latency Global Switch
     eliminates the programming complexity and performance penalties 
     associated with NUMA architectures."

	If the 3:1 maximum latency ratio (quoted in another thread)
	is correct, then this is again the same as the SGI Origin2000.
	The Origin2000 shows 2:1 latency ratios and 1.2:1 sustained
	bandwidth ratios for small configurations, growing to 3:1 latency
	and 2:1 bandwidth ratios for worst-case communication in a large
	(128 cpu) system.

	Unfortunately, building good hardware is just the beginning of
	the trouble with getting excellent performance out of a NUMA
	system.  Some things are easy, some are hard, and some are 
	impossible if you did not build in the hooks to bypass the
	cache coherence protocol.  (SGI did not build in those hooks.)


    "SKC believes that a fully-configured GS320 could achieve 140K to 
     150K tpmC, thus besting the Sun UE10000's ~115K tpmC benchmark
     and the IBM S80's anticipated ~120K tpmC results. Pricing remains 
     uncertain, but we believe a top-of-the-line GS320 will go for less 
     than $2M, about $1M less than a 64-CPU Sun UE10K server."

	IBM's current posted result is 135,816 with the S80, which is
	priced at under $1M for the 24-way base configuration (about $1.5M
	for the 64 GB configuration used in the TPC-C result (sans disk).  

-- 
John D. McCalpin, Ph.D.           mccalpin@austin.ibm.com
Senior Scientist                  IBM Future Systems
