Paul DeMone wrote:
> 
> "John D. McCalpin, Ph.D." wrote:
> >
> > This looks like an SGI Origin2000, just 3.5 years later and not
> > as scalable....
> 
>    Scalable as in number of CPUs ?
> 
>    Yes but these are Alpha EV67 CPUs not MIPS R10K retreads.
> 
>    It is easier pulling a heavy load harnessing 32 clydesdales than
>    128 goats.

So is anyone surprised that a machine introduced in early 2000 is
faster than a machine introduced in the last quarter of 1996?

The point was (and is) that the architectural concept is neither
new nor unique.  

If SGI had not cancelled the H1/Beast processor, they would be at 
approximate parity with WildFire on a per-cpu basis for floating-point 
workloads (i.e. SN1/Beast/666 MHz vs WildFire/EV67/700 MHz).  I don't
recall the exact numbers, but the "Beast" should have been within
about 20% of the SPECfp95 performance of the EV67/700.

As it is, the 400 MHz R12000 shrink will be a credible performer in 
the SN1 frame for floating-point workloads.  It will be slower than 
the EV67 (duh...), but it is likely to offer comparable throughput 
at each price point (assuming Terry Shannon is correct about pricing 
of the WildFire system).

I have more detailed performance comparisons, but I will let SGI
release that data when they are ready.  A fixed throughput comparison 
between WildFire and SN1/MIPS/400 is likely to have a processor ratio 
of less than 1:2 (depending on what L2 cache size SGI ends up shipping),
not 1:4 as was implied above.

