In article <82a606$q6v$1@odo.ecs.umass.edu>, <heimann@ecs.umass.edu>
wrote:

>We have several DEC 3000 machines set up as servers running DU 4.0D.
>Using vdump to backup filesystems I have noticed two bugs, one minor,
>the other a bit more serious.  I am curious if these have been fixed,
>either by patches or in later releases.  First the minor bug which is
>mostly cosmetic.  When vdump starts running, several lines down in
>its reporting is a line that goes:
>
>      vdump: Date of last level 'n' dump: 'date'
>
>The 'n' it will report is the same as the dump level being done, 9
>for a level 9 dump and so on.  However that date will be one for the
>most recent dump of the highest number lower than the current dump,
>0 or 2 or whatever.  Mildly annoying, but only a problem when a new
>operator reports it as a problem.  It has been there for some time,
>I don't recall which version I first saw this in.


My sources tell me that this problem has not been reported.  I suggest
that if you would like a fix, that you contact the telephone support
center (or your customer representative) and ask them to escalate the
problem with an IPMT.


>The second bug shows up sometimes when a vdump is aborted after a
>Ctrl-C to interrupt it, usually because of a bad tape or the drive
>needs to be cleaned.  Even though the dump was aborted, it will about
>half the time write the date and time stamp to /etc/vdumpdates as if
>there was a successful completion.  I can go out and edit the file to
>put the correct date stamp in for whatever dump level was aborted, but
>the incorrect date does not always get noticed and it throws off later
>dumps some of the time.


I'm told that this was fixed (at least in a later release).  What I do
not know is if the fix is part of any existing patches.  You could
check the patch kit release notes to see if this problem is mentioned,
or you could ask the Telephone Support Center to escalate this as well
via an IPMT



