X-Trace: news1-hme0 944799521 195.44.3.153 (Fri, 10 Dec 1999 04:18:41 GMT)
MIME-Version: 1.0
NNTP-Posting-Date: Fri, 10 Dec 1999 04:18:41 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

I've been built enlightenment 0.16.3 on an Alpha under Tru64 4.0e.
Ran into two problems that I have patches for, and a third problem
with a workaround.

BTW - enlightenment's great - impresses the hell out people I give
demos to.  I've set aside the native Tru64 CDE.

Patch 1:-------------------------
Symptom: eesh runs.  On exit, the parent shell/xterm exit as well.
What's happening:  eesh sets O_NONBLOCK on stdin.  Under Tru64,
this can permanently affect parent's pty.  The parent (interactive)
shell
barfs.  The patch (context diff on 9-Dec-99 CVS source) restores the
state on exit.  Tested on Tru64 4.0e+patchkit2 and Redhat 6.0 i386.
I'd recommend this for addition to the source.

~/CVS/enlightenment[15] diff -c e/eesh/main.c{_orig,}
*** e/eesh/main.c_orig  Fri Dec 10 03:17:35 1999
--- e/eesh/main.c       Fri Dec 10 03:19:56 1999
***************
*** 30,35 ****
--- 30,40 ----
  
  extern char         waitonly;
  
+ static int stdin_state;
+ void restore_stdin_state(void) {
+    fcntl(0, F_SETFL, stdin_state);
+ }
+ 
  int
  main(int argc, char **argv)
  {
***************
*** 112,117 ****
--- 117,124 ----
  
     XSync(disp, False);
     j = 0;
+    stdin_state = fcntl(0, F_GETFL, 0);
+    atexit( restore_stdin_state );
     fcntl(0, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK);
     for (;;)
       {


Patch 2:-------------------------
Symptom: Menu items using "exec" don't work (most of them).  On rare
occasions it succeeds.  Enlightenment pops up the dialog containing
the phrase "I suggest you read the manual page for that shell and 
read up how to change or add to your execution path".

What's happening:  Looks like a weird race condition in actions.c,
not an enlightenment bug.  The ExecApplication call does a popen()
and the first fread() on the pipe "succeeds" in reading 0 bytes,
as if it were non-blocking (non-blocking is not set).  Either
a 40msec pause or secondary fread() will succeed.  Don't know
why - closed source OS.  I'm submitting a bug report to Compaq/Dec,
but here's a workaround patch that seems to work.
Not recommended for CVS :-), though the patch is also against
