Marc Baudoin <babafou@pasteur.fr> wrote:
> Allan Hawdon <udaa460@axolotl.kcl.ac.uk> �crit :
>> I've been running sys_check on an ES40 with Tru64 v4.0F and
>> one of its recommendations is to enable fsck on / & /usr in fstab.
>> These are advfs filesystems and I'm wary of this as I have the 
>> feeling that fsck is only for ufs filesystems. Am I just being silly
>> here?

> There is no tool to make a damaged AdvFS file domain consistent
> again.  Compaq ships something called "verify" but it's still
> experimental and it does not guarantee to get your file domain
> back.  So for filesystems I don't need specific features only
> found in AdvFS, I still use UFS. This is especially true for /,
> /usr and /var, which are important filesystems.  I always use
> UFS for them because fsck is always able (well, in 99 % of the
> cases, which is not bad compared to verify) to get your
> filesystem consistent again (you might lose some files in the
> process, but it's quite rare as these filesystems don't have much
> writing activity).

I have all machines here configured with AdvFS only. No UFS.
Starting with 4.0E I consider AdvFS to be stable enough for a production
environment. There's another tool called "salvage" which can repair
AdvFS domains. But I've never needed it. It is true that there were
problems with AdvFS domains in DU <= 4.0D but my problems were mostly
related to faulty hardware.

Apart from that: Before upgrading or patching a system, Compaq
recommends to run /sbin/advfs/verify on all domains, just to be 100%
sure that they are intact. Of course there's no fsck for AdvFs.

Tom
