In article <ldzrAZ3BmPXf@malvm2.mala.bc.ca>, Malcolm Dunnett
<dunnett@mala.bc.ca> wrote:

>  I have a system running Digital Unix 4.0D
>
>  On that system I have an ADVFs domain ( called system ) currently 
>consisting of a single 9GB disk ( /dev/rz6c ). The volume is about
>99% full. Yesterday I tried to add a partition of another disk 
>(/dev/rz1g) into this domain using:
>
>        addvol /dev/rz1g system
>
> and I got the error:
>
>addvol: error = E_TOO_MANY_VIRTUAL_DISKS (-1145)
>addvol: Can't add volume '/dev/rz1g' to domain 'system'
>
>  The man page advfs_err(4) says this means this domain already has
>the maximum number of disks allowed for a domain. What is stopping
>this domain from having more than one disk?
>
>  note: contrary to its name, the "system" volume doesn't have the
>/ or /usr partitions, it only contains user files.

It is my guess that somehow this domain got created with the mkfdmn -r
option.  The -r options is for creating the root filesystem which is
not allowed to have more than one volume because it is difficult to
find all the volumes for root if the /etc/fdmns directory is not on the
disk that is specified with the boot command :-)

Anyway, like I said, I suspect that this is the problem.  If it is,
then the only thing you can do is to create a new domain (without -r)
and vdump/vrestore your data to the newly created domain.

You can check to see if the disk was created with mkfdmn -r with the
following command:

    /sbin/advfs/vbmtpg /dev/rz6c | grep maxVds

If the value says

    maxVds 1

then the domain must have been created using mkfdmn -r.  Otherwise the
value should say

    maxVds 256

If it says 256, then there is someother problem.

In either event, you still may need to create a new domain and
vdump/vrestore your data on to the new domain.



